if you're thinking about becoming an  architect there's lots of pros and cons  today i want to go through some of my  personal experience and  my thoughts  [Music]  what's going on guys my name is david  thomas and i'm a registered architect in  the state of western australia  if you're new here firstly thanks so  much for joining me on this channel we  talk about technology and architecture  so if that's something that interests  you make sure you smash that subscribe  button down below  like i mentioned today we're talking  about the pros and the cons of becoming  an architect  there are many on each side of the  battle but we want to start with the  pros today  so architecture is a very long road that  starts out at university  university is one of the craziest and  busiest times in your  whole architectural career in my  personal opinion because it is  just so full-on there is continuous  all-nighters for projects to get them  out the door  i think one of my worst was 48 hours  awake straight just to get a couple  projects out and i was somebody that was  extremely good with time management  so the fact that i was still up 48 hours  means everybody else was up for 50  60 70 hours straight to get some of  these major projects done  university is killer but it is an  amazing time you meet some amazing  people that will be lifelong friends and  colleagues  all the way through your professional  career as an architect you quickly  become the jack of all trades in  creativity  you're a completely creative person that  spends their time  just thinking abstractly and outside the  box most of the time you're never just  focused on one thing  you always have to juggle a few hence  the jack of all trades  eventually you become a little bit of a  master at engineering a little bit of  architecture  a little bit civil a little bit  structural and it just kind of mixes and  the flow comes together  everybody knows that the architect knows  almost everything  but also needs a lot of assistance so  don't get a big head as an architect  just because you've worked on a lot of  different projects  and know what should happen you still  should have to rely  on the professionals that you work with  if you think you were never bored at  university you'll also never get bored  in the real world  you're always juggling hundreds of  projects across one year  it's usually about 10 projects at any  given time  so for example you might be working on  five houses  three commercial buildings and two fit  outs they're all completely different  they all have different demands all have  different deadlines  so you're always under the pump trying  to juggle all these projects and  trying to satisfy everybody i know that  sounds like a bit of a con but we'll get  to that later  it is genuinely just a good time that  you're never bored at work because you  constantly have to think of other things  and different projects  in australia the word architect is a  protected title you have to be a  registered architect in australia to be  able to call yourself an architect  otherwise it is an offense so if  prestige honor and title are something  that you enjoy  then having the title of an architect is  definitely noteworthy  as an architect you get to improve many  many people's lives  everybody that comes into your office is  most of the time looking to better  themselves or their business  so in some way shape or form you are  pivotal in the next stage of their life  no matter what type of client walks  through your door they're all going to  be different  so you could have 10 identical projects  theoretically 10 residential houses  but every single client from their age  to their demographic  to their income is going to be  completely different and they're going  to need to be traded differently  so you're going to meet a whole range of  diverse people which is always exciting  to see the different walks of life  because you will have so many projects  most of the time you're going to have to  travel  quite a bit personally i have to travel  over a thousand kilometers every couple  of weeks  just to visit all of my clients so it is  very important  that you do enjoy travel and you're okay  with being away from home  otherwise this pro becomes a con very  quickly and i guess my final pro as  being an architect is that you're in  charge of a whole team  usually the architect is the leader of  the project it is on them  to navigate to manage and to coordinate  the whole process so you'll be directed  and directing at the same time  it is a hat that you juggle it is  another jack-of-all-trades skill that  you have to master  so you become very good at time  management you become very good at  navigating the minefield of people's  opinions  and you also know what opinions matter  to you and how you can progress with  those opinions  overall personally i still do believe  the pros outweigh the cons as an  architect  which i'm about to get onto now because  obviously i wouldn't still be an  architect  if i didn't believe that the pros  outweighed the cons  so moving on to the cons and starting  out where everybody does university  if you go to university to become an  architect sit there in day one and look  at your class  look at them very well because there is  a good chance 80 percent of that class  will not make it as an architect i  started my university degree with 600  students  alongside me by the time i got to my  fifth year and completed my master's  there was only about 40 people that  graduated alongside me  and to this day many years after i don't  keep in touch with that many people  so this statement is somewhat untrue but  the people i do keep in touch with i'm  the only registered architect at this  point in time  so having said that one from 600 people  is a very very tough gig to get through  and when you finally do find the job as  a graduate architect  which keeping in mind it is extremely  competitive because the guys that did  graduate  and girls are exceptionally good at  their jobs  so getting a job as a graduate architect  is incredibly difficult  then you go into the industry and you're  only making somewhere around the 50 000  mark in australia that barely covers  living expenses  so 50 000 is a very poor salary to start  on  nonetheless if you are good at your job  and you're able to prove yourself  your salary will go up so this isn't the  worst thing in the world  i know many blogs and many forums say  architects don't make a lot of money and  there is  very little chance of getting rich  however i find that  difficult to believe if you aren't good  at your job if you're good at your job  in any profession you'll make money and  you'll do well  so just keep that in mind when you read  all those blogs in all those forums  you might think you've graduated  university you've done your time you've  got a job  now you're an architect oh no there's  still a long road to go before you can  call yourself an architect  so another con of being an architect is  the journey to becoming an architect  is minimum seven to eight years  depending on how quickly you can finish  sections of your requirements and in  that seven to eight years it is  extremely stressful especially once you  come into the real world and into the  work life  work is always stressful there is 10 or  so jobs that you're juggling  at any given time like i mentioned  before there is hundreds of people  constantly emailing you asking you  questions  calling you at every time of day and  night it is  very very stressful but you do become  good at managing stress and managing  your time  and i guess the stress really comes down  to deadlines and the risk factor  you have constant deadlines from clients  saying i need this job done now i need  this job done now i need this job done  now i need this job done now  and you're just trying to please  everybody by getting it out to them as  quickly as possible  the risk as well is the fact that you  might miss something it might go to  construction  it might not meet the codes and  something might go horribly wrong  so it is your responsibility to make  sure everything is correct  in any set of documents that is leaving  your office to go for construction  now another big con of the architecture  profession is clients might walk into  your door  sign up get their first set of drawings  and then never pay  you could have done hours and hours and  hours of work and never see the  financial reward out of that and i guess  my final con for the day is the simple  fact that you are always criticized  architecture is the subjective field it  doesn't matter what you design  somebody's gonna hate it  so if you're uncomfortable with being  criticized if you're uncomfortable  with getting feedback that isn't always  positive  then unfortunately architecture probably  isn't for you  because as architects wake up criticism  every single day  there's award-winning designs out there  that people have hated  especially the very first one your  client sees the first presentation to  your client  trying to figure out what they really  want is very difficult and nailing that  the first time doesn't usually happen so  you have to be willing to take that  criticism on board and make things  better just because that's what that  person  truly wants anyway that's all for me  today guys thank you so much for  watching i hope you enjoyed the video  if you did make sure you smash that  subscribe button down below if you want  to know anything more about becoming an  architect or the architecture profession  leave a comment i'm happy to reply i try  to reply to as many people as possible  now because this video forms one of my  28 videos in 28 days  it would usually be i'll see you next  monday but in this case  i'll see you tomorrow

